/-!
# The quotient `ℱ/Iℱ` is acyclic above the dimension of the zero locus of `I`

`IdealMultiplesCohomology.lean` reduces the vanishing of `Hᵖ(X, ℱ)` to two inputs: the acyclicity
of the single quotient `ℱ/Iℱ` in degree `p`, and one degree of vanishing on an auxiliary kernel.
This file supplies the first of them.

The quotient `ℱ/Iℱ` — the cokernel of `ψ : ⨁_{a ∈ I} ℱ ⟶ ℱ` — is killed by `I`, so its stalk
vanishes at every point where some element of `I` is invertible, i.e. everywhere off the zero
locus of `I`. Grothendieck vanishing in the support form,
`Stacks.CohomologyVanishing.isZero_rightDerived_moduleGlobalSections_of_forall_stalk_isZero`,
then kills its cohomology above the dimension of any closed Noetherian set containing that zero
locus.

In the situation of [Stacks, Tag 02V7] over a local base, `I` is generated by the maximal ideal
of the base ring, its zero locus is the closed fibre, and the conclusion is the acyclicity of
`ℱ/𝔪ℱ` above the dimension of that fibre.

## Main results

All in the namespace `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.TopDegreeBaseChange`.

* `isZero_stalk_of_smul_id_eq_zero`: a sheaf of modules killed by a global function has vanishing
  stalk at every point of the basic open of that function.
* `smul_id_cokernel_idealMultiples_eq_zero`: `ℱ/Iℱ` is killed by every element of `I`.
* `isZero_stalk_cokernel_idealMultiples`: hence its stalks vanish off the zero locus of `I`.
* `isZero_rightDerived_cokernel_idealMultiples_of_dim_le` and
  `isZero_rightDerivedPlus_cokernel_idealMultiples_of_dim_le`: **the acyclicity of `ℱ/Iℱ` above
  the dimension of the zero locus of `I`**, which is the hypothesis `hcoker` of
  `epi_map_rightDerived_idealMultiples`, `top_le_smul_top_rightDerived` and
  `isZero_rightDerived_of_isZero_cokernel`.
* `baseMaximalIdeal` and `exists_mem_baseMaximalIdeal_mem_basicOpen`: for a morphism to the
  spectrum of a local ring, the ideal of global functions generated by the maximal ideal, and
  the invertibility of one of its elements at each point off the closed fibre.
* `isZero_rightDerived_cokernel_idealMultiples_baseMaximalIdeal`: **the acyclicity of `ℱ/𝔪ℱ`
  above the dimension of the closed fibre**, for a proper morphism onto the spectrum of a
  Noetherian local ring. This is the form [Stacks, Tag 02V7] consumes.

## Implementation notes

**Nothing is quasi-coherent, and there is no morphism of schemes.** The statements are about an
arbitrary sheaf of modules on an arbitrary scheme; the closed set is a parameter, asked only to
be Noetherian of bounded dimension and to contain the zero locus. A geometric input — a proper
morphism onto an affine base, with `I` generated by an ideal of the base ring — supplies the
closed set as a fibre, and nothing in the proof needs to know that.

**Why the hypothesis is `∃ a ∈ I, x ∈ X.basicOpen a` rather than a scheme-theoretic zero locus.**
The locus where a module killed by `I` can fail to vanish is the common zero locus of the
sections in `I`, and membership in the complement is exactly invertibility of some `a ∈ I` at the
point. Stated this way the hypothesis is checked pointwise and needs no ideal sheaf.

A section killed by a global function `a` vanishes over every open contained in `X.basicOpen a`:
there `a` restricts to a unit, because each of its germs is one by the definition of the basic
open, which is `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSpace.isUnit_of_isUnit_germ`. An arbitrary stalk element
is the germ of a section over some open, and `TopCat.Presheaf.germ_res_apply` moves it into the
intersection of that open with the basic open.

The action of `a : Γ(X, ⊤)` on local sections is `Stacks.SheafOfModules.smul_val_app`:
multiplication by the restriction of `a`. The restriction map is written with `homOfLE`, while
the one that lemma produces comes from `isTerminalTop`; the morphisms of `(Opens X)ᵒᵖ` are
unique, so `Subsingleton.elim` identifies them — the same step
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/ZeroSchemeAnnihilation/Basic.lean` takes. The scalar is spelled at
`X.ringCatSheaf.obj.map`, which is where the `Module` instance on `ℱ.val.obj (op V)` is keyed,
and the unit is produced at `X.presheaf.map`, which is where the basic-open API lives; the two
terms are the same element of the same carrier.

The `Linear ↥Γ(X, ⊤) X.Modules` instance is the one declared in
`IdealMultiplesCohomology.lean`.

